d_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":-0.0632577871247189,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gate_share_shrink":0.11219732542700256,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":1.7464309993201903,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.01403,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":9.4,"_gate_insider_buying":0.2,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":20.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Ocugen, Inc., a biopharmaceutical company, focuses on discovering, developing, and commercializing novel gene and cell therapies, biologic, and vaccines that improve patients' health. The company's pipeline product includes OCU400, a novel gene therapy product candidate restoring retinal integrity and function across a range of genetically diverse inherited retinal diseases, currently under Phase 3 trials for the treatment of retinitis pigmentosa and Phase 1/2 trials for the treatment of leber congenital amaurosis; OCU410, a gene therapy under phase 1/2 for the treatment of dry age-related macular degeneration (AMD); and OCU410ST, a gene therapy under phase 1/2 for the treatment of Stargardt disease. It is involved in the development of OCU200, a novel recombinant fusion protein for the treatment of diabetic macular edema, diabetic retinopathy, and wet age-related macular degeneration; and NeoCart, an autologous chondrocyte-derived neocartilage for the repair of knee cartilage injuries in adult. In addition, the company is developing OCU500, a COVID-19 vaccine; OCU510, a seasonal quadrivalent flu vaccine; and OCU520, a combination quadrivalent seasonal flu and COVID-19 vaccine. It has collaboration agreements with National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases for early clinical studies for the OCU500 program; and a strategic partnership with CanSino Biologics Inc. for manufacturing its modifier gene therapy pipeline product candidates. The company was founded in 2013 and is headquartered in Malvern, Pennsylvania.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Shankar  Musunuri M.B.A., Ph.D. | Co-Founder, CEO & Chairman | Age 61 | Compensation: $1.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 116 people. Revenue per employee of $38K ranks in the bottom quartile among Healthcare peers. Stock-based compensation of $66K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 33\u00d7 revenue per employee (elevated relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["Ocugen, Inc. is a small-cap healthcare business with a $314M market cap on $4M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +370% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at -241% against a 11% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The company is burning through $57M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","Accruals are elevated at -25.0% of assets, and Sloan showed firms building earnings through accruals rather than cash systematically underperform.","On the balance sheet, thin -12.1\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Net dilution runs at 6.3% annually, transferring value from owners to stock-based comp, and stock-based comp at 175% of revenue is silent dilution an owner has to account for, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 2/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (-14.4) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short intere